    'We formulate the rehabilitation task as a pursuit-evasion game played on a flat workspace. The patient sits in front of the acrylic surface and uses one hand to chase the magnetic microrobot, which moves under the control of the robot arm below. The goal is to catch the microrobot by bringing the hand within a small contact radius. Unlike static reaching exercises, the microrobot actively evades the patient\'s hand. Catching it therefore requires continuous visuomotor tracking, trajectory prediction, and real-time motor planning--sustained cognitive and physical demands that go beyond point-to-point reaching. Clinical evidence shows that continuous, goal-directed visuomotor tracking engages the central nervous system and promotes neuroplasticity for motor recovery [X]. To maximize therapeutic benefit, task difficulty must adapt to the patient\'s current ability. This need for adaptive difficulty is formalized by the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD), a concept from educational psychology introduced by Vygotsky [X] that describes the range of tasks a learner can perform with guidance but not alone. In our setting, the ZPD translates to a target distance range between the hand and the microrobot: the robot should neither escape completely nor yield passively, but keep the distance within this range so the patient stays challenged without becoming fatigued or frustrated. Achieving this within the ZPD is non-trivial, as the robot must continuously respond to a patient whose movement speed, strategy, and motor noise may all vary.',

