    'The simulation study is organized around the main sources of uncertainty that the controller must handle before physical deployment. We first examine whether iterative league training produces a robot policy that remains effective across a growing set of learned hand behaviors. We then isolate the role of temporal interaction history and auxiliary future-dynamics supervision through a controlled network ablation. Finally, we compare representative robot policies under two automated hand-control mechanisms to assess generalization across pursuit strategies.',
